the next step is to get the current Deployment or StatefulSet YAML to see the volumes. Let me check the available tools. The exec_kubectl_cmd_safely can run "kubectl get deployment" or "kubectl get statefulset" commands. So the user should first check which controller is managing the pod. For example, run "kubectl get pod url-shorten-mongodb -n social-network -o jsonpath=\'{.metadata.ownerReferences}\'" to find the controller. But that might be complex. Alternatively, they can check the pod\'s YAML to see the controller.\n\nAlternatively, the user can proceed to check the current volumes in the pod\'s spec. But since the pod is managed by a controller, the volumes are defined in the controller\'s spec. So the user needs to check the controller\'s YAML.\n\nBut given the tools available, the user can use exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to run "kubectl describe pod url-shorten-mongodb -n social-network" to get the pod\'s details, including the volumes. Wait, the user already did that. The previous steps showed that the pod\'s volumes include the ConfigMap but not the Secret. So the next step is to modify the controller to add the Secret as a volume.\n\nBut how to do that? The user needs to edit the Deployment or StatefulSet. For example, if it\'s a Deployment, they can run "kubectl edit deployment url-shorten-mongodb -n social-network" to add the secret as a volume. But the tool doesn\'t support interactive commands. So the user needs to apply a patch using kubectl apply.\n\nBut the user needs to know the exact name of the Deployment or StatefulSet. Let\'s assume that the pod is part of a Deployment called "url-shorten-mongodb-deployment" or similar. Alternatively, maybe the StatefulSet is named "url-shorten-mongodb-statefulset".\n\nAlternatively, the user can check the pod\'s owner reference. So using exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to run "kubectl get pod url-shorten-mongodb -n social-network -o jsonpath=\'{.metadata.ownerReferences}\'" to find the controller. But that might not be straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, the user can proceed to check the current volumes in the Deployment or StatefulSet. For example, run "kubectl get deployment url-shorten-mongodb -n social-network -o yaml" to see the volumes. If the Deployment is not found, check for a StatefulSet.\n\nBut given the time constraints, the user should proceed to modify the controller to add the secret. So the next step is to use exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to apply a patch to the Deployment or StatefulSet to add the secret as a volume. However, the user needs to know the exact name of the controller. If they can\'t find it, they might need to list all Deployments and StatefulSets in the namespace.\n\nBut the user might not have that information. So perhaps the correct approach is to first check the current configuration of the pod\'s controller. Therefore, the next tool call should be to get the Deployment or StatefulSet YAML to check the volumes. Let\'s proceed with that.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\ntool call: {"command": "kubectl describe pod url-shorten-mongodb -n social-network"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1430, 'prompt_tokens': 2090, 'total_tokens': 3520, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbbf-93a1-7b13-b679-916932c02bc1-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2090, 'output_tokens': 1430, 'total_tokens': 3520}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='eec1b924-f8e3-40b9-b813-ab4778b158a8'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is dealing with a MongoDB pod that's crashing with a CrashLoopBackOff status. From th