9e6b2a5c981dc8e8'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='56244c39-18df-4c5c-a2ea-7a70cc474683'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is dealing with a MongoDB pod that\'s crashing with CrashLoopBackOff. From the previous step, they used kubectl describe pod and saw that the container is crashing. The next step is to figure out why the container is failing. The error message mentions that the container exited with code 1, which usually indicates an error in the startup process.\n\nLooking at the pod\'s configuration, the MongoDB container is using a config file located at /social-network-microservices/config/mongod.conf. The user needs to check what\'s in that config file. If the config file is missing or misconfigured, that could cause MongoDB to fail. But wait, the user already checked the ConfigMap and found that the ConfigMap is mounted. However, the error might be related to TLS settings since the previous step mentioned a missing TLS certificate.\n\nWait, the user mentioned that the ConfigMap is using a secret. Let me check the previous steps again. Oh right, the user found that the ConfigMap references a secret (mongodb-tls) for the TLS certificate. But the secret isn\'t mounted into the pod. The pod\'s volume mounts include the ConfigMap but not the Secret. So the MongoDB container can\'t find the TLS certificate because the secret isn\'t mounted to /etc/tls/ or wherever it\'s expected.\n\nSo the next step is to verify the pod\'s volume mounts to see if the secret is attached. The user should check the pod\'s spec to see if the secret is mounted. If not, they need to update the Deployment or StatefulSet that manages the MongoDB pod to include the secret as a volume mount. \n\nBut how to check the pod\'s volume mounts? The user can use kubectl describe pod to see the volumes. Wait, they already did that. The output shows that the pod has a ConfigMap mounted but no mention of the secret. So the secret isn\'t mounted. Therefore, the solution is to add the secret as a volume in the pod\'s configuration. \n\nBut the user can\'t modify the pod directly; they need to update the Deployment. So the next tool call should be to exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to describe the Deployment or StatefulSet that manages the MongoDB pod. Let me check the pod\'s owner reference. The pod is controlled by a ReplicaSet, which is part of a Deployment. So the user should describe the Deployment to see the volume configurations. \n\nAlternatively, they can check the pod\'s YAML to see the volumes. But using kubectl describe pod might not show the full YAML. So the best approach is to get the pod\'s YAML specification to check the volumes. Therefore, the next tool call should be to exec_kubectl_cmd_safely with "kubectl get pod url-shorten-mongodb-ccd7bb58d-nbvbw -n social-network -o yaml" to get the detailed configuration and check the volumes section. That way, they can confirm if the secret is mounted and where.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\ntool call: {"command": "kubectl get pod url-shorten-mongodb-ccd7bb58d-nbvbw -n social-network -o yaml"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 657, 'prompt_tokens': 3318, 'total_tokens': 3975, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbbf-eb87-7b53-a6b3-22cd8aa727f0-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3318, 'output_tokens': 657, 'total_tokens': 3975}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='81967215-2fd8-4214-b6b5-b749a2f6bf10'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is dealing with a MongoDB pod that's crashing with a CrashLoopBackOff status. From the previous steps, they checked the pod's logs and description, found that the container is crashing because it can't find the TLS certificate. They looked into the ConfigMap and found that the MongoDB configuration points to /etc/tls/tls.pem. Then they checked the Secrets in the namespace and found a mongodb-tls secret that has tls.pem, but when they described the secret, it shows that the data is there.\n\nWait, but the problem is that the secret isn't mounted into the pod. The ConfigMap is using the tls.pem from the secret, but the pod's volumes aren't set up to mount the secret. So even though the secret exists, the container doesn't have access to it because it's not mounted. The user needs to check the pod's volume mounts to see if the secret is attached. \n\nBut the user already used kubectl describe pod and saw that the volume mounts include the ConfigMap but not the Secret. The ConfigMap is mounted, but the Secret isn't. So the solution would be to update the pod's spec to include the secret as a volume mount. However, the user can't modify the pod directly; they need to update the Deployment or StatefulSet that's managing it. \n\nSince the user is supposed to submit the root cause, they should explain that the mongodb-tls secret exists but isn't mounted to /etc/tls/, causing MongoDB to fail. The user should be informed to mount the secret in the pod's configuration.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pod url-shorten-mongodb-ccd7bb58d-nbvbw -n social-network -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9e1bc00800fa6f06', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 388, 'prompt_tokens': 4387, 'total_tokens': 4775, 'completion_t