, id='2df43bae-a044-4fa1-8231-91256f805959', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a7f431c3e312a370'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='580458a6-f983-48e7-b6c1-aa19d65a78bd'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The MongoDB pod is crashing because of a CrashLoopBackOff. From the previous steps, the user checked the pod\'s logs and found that the container can\'t find the TLS certificate. The ConfigMap \'url-shorten-mongodb\' has a reference to /etc/tls/tls.pem, but when they checked the Secret \'mongodb-tls\', it exists with the tls.pem file. However, the pod\'s volume mounts don\'t include the Secret. The ConfigMap is mounted, but the Secret isn\'t. So the issue is that the Secret isn\'t mounted into the pod, causing MongoDB to fail when it tries to access the TLS certificate.\n\nThe next step is to ensure that the Secret is mounted into the pod. The user needs to check the Deployment or StatefulSet that manages the MongoDB pod. Since the pod is using a ConfigMap, maybe the Deployment is configured to use both the ConfigMap and the Secret. But in the current setup, the Secret isn\'t mounted. \n\nThe correct action is to update the pod\'s configuration to include the Secret as a volume mount. Since the user can\'t modify the pod directly, they should edit the Deployment or StatefulSet. The command would involve updating the Deployment to add the Secret as a volume and mount it to /etc/tls/. \n\nSo the next tool call should be to exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to edit the Deployment. The user needs to apply the changes to mount the Secret. After making the change, they should wait a few seconds for the pod to restart and check if the issue is resolved. If the pod starts successfully, the problem is fixed. If not, they might need to check the logs again or adjust the mount path.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\ntool call: {"command": "kubectl edit deployment url-shorten-mongodb -n social-network"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 388, 'prompt_tokens': 5501, 'total_tokens': 5889, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbc0-7e4d-7da3-bbb8-15aaf1a59678-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5501, 'output_tokens': 388, 'total_tokens': 5889}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='904c3a93-50e1-42fc-ae17-370b6a25b83a'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is dealing with a MongoDB pod that's crashing with a CrashLoopBackOff status. From the previous steps, they checked the pod's logs and description, found that the container is crashing because it can't find the TLS certificate. They looked into the ConfigMap and found that the MongoDB configuration points to /etc/tls/tls.pem. Then they checked the Secrets in the namespace and found a mongodb-tls secret that has tls.pem, but when they described the secret, it shows that the data is there.\n\nWait, but the problem is that the secret isn't mounted into the pod. The ConfigMap is using the tls.pem from the secret, but the pod's volumes aren't set up to mount the secret. So even though the secret exists, the container doesn't have access to it because it's not mounted. The user needs to check the pod's volume mounts to see if the secret is attached. \n\nBut the user already used kubectl describe pod and saw that the volume mounts include the ConfigMap but not the Secret. The ConfigMap is mounted, but the Secret isn't. So the solution would be to update the pod's spec to include the secret as a volume mount. However, the user can't modify the pod directly; they need to update the Deployment or StatefulSet that's managing it. \n\nSince the user is supposed to submit the root cause, they should explain that the mongodb-tls secret exists but isn't mounted to /etc/tls/, causing MongoDB to fail. The user should be informed to mount the secret in the pod's configuration.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl edit deployment url-shorten-mongodb -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-85a5ff3950790418', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 375, 'prompt_tokens': 6557, 'total_tokens': 6932, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbc0-90b4-7ad0-a87c-5ef12a0e7996-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl edit deployment url-shorten-mongodb -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-85a5ff3950790418', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6557, 'output_tokens': 375, 'total_tokens': 6932}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected (ValueError): Interactive command kubectl edit detected. Such commands are not supported.', id='8c873644-358f-4059-98d2-be1d8c0257ad', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-85a5ff3950790418'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='02e92114-6015-49a6-94d7-71f1f7f35177')]]
