 to /etc/tls/ or wherever it\'s expected.\n\nSo the next step is to verify the pod\'s volume mounts to see if the secret is attached. The user should check the pod\'s spec to see if the secret is mounted. If not, they need to update the Deployment or StatefulSet that manages the MongoDB pod to include the secret as a volume mount. \n\nBut how to check the pod\'s volume mounts? The user can use kubectl describe pod to see the volumes. Wait, they already did that. The output shows that the pod has a ConfigMap mounted but no mention of the secret. So the secret isn\'t mounted. Therefore, the solution is to add the secret as a volume in the pod\'s configuration. \n\nBut the user can\'t modify the pod directly; they need to update the Deployment. So the next tool call should be to exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to describe the Deployment or StatefulSet that manages the MongoDB pod. Let me check the pod\'s owner reference. The pod is controlled by a ReplicaSet, which is part of a Deployment. So the user should describe the Deployment to see the volume configurations. \n\nAlternatively, they can check the pod\'s YAML to see the volumes. But using kubectl describe pod might not show the full YAML. So the best approach is to get the pod\'s YAML specification to check the volumes. Therefore, the next tool call should be to exec_kubectl_cmd_safely with "kubectl get pod url-shorten-mongodb-ccd7bb58d-nbvbw -n social-network -o yaml" to get the detailed configuration and check the volumes section. That way, they can confirm if the secret is mounted and where.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\ntool call: {"command": "kubectl get pod url-shorten-mongodb-ccd7bb58d-nbvbw -n social-network -o yaml"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 657, 'prompt_tokens': 3318, 'total_tokens': 3975, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen